(function(){var I=window.CLF_I18N=window.CLF_I18N||{};var L=I.en=I.en||{};['q','course','lessons','glossary','services','domains','ui'].forEach(function(k){L[k]=L[k]||{};});
Object.assign(L.q,{
"d1-40":{exp:"An AZ is one or more separate data centers with redundant, independent power, networking, and connectivity. The AZs in a Region are linked by low-latency networking, so running across multiple AZs is easy. Regions are isolated from each other. But AWS does NOT replicate your resources for you: you have to design for multi-AZ yourself.",why:["Correct: AZs have separate power, networking, and connectivity, so one failure does not take down the other AZs.","Correct: The network linking the AZs has low latency and high bandwidth, which lets you synchronize data between AZs.","Correct: Regions are designed to be isolated, which limits how far a failure can spread.","Incorrect: AWS does not copy EC2 instances automatically. The customer has to run instances in multiple AZs (for example, with an Auto Scaling group spread across several AZs).","Incorrect: Edge locations serve CloudFront, Route 53, and similar services to reduce latency for users. They are not used to run a primary database.","Incorrect: AZs are physically separated (by many kilometers) precisely so that a problem in one building does not affect all of them."]},
"d1-41":{exp:"Keywords: long-term growth, adding capacity as demand grows → scalability. Elasticity emphasizes scaling up and down automatically with short-term swings. HA and fault tolerance are about surviving failures. Durability is about not losing data.",why:["Correct: Scalability is the ability to add capacity to handle growing load without a redesign.","Incorrect: High availability means keeping the system running with minimal downtime when something fails. It is not about serving growth.","Incorrect: Fault tolerance means continuing to operate when a component fails. The question does not mention failures.","Incorrect: Durability means data is not lost over time (for example, in S3). It has nothing to do with processing capacity."]},
"d1-42":{exp:"Data residency: the customer chooses the Region (for example, eu-central-1 in Frankfurt). AWS does not move customer content out of the chosen Region unless the customer does it themselves (for example, by turning on cross-Region replication).",why:["Correct: Choosing the Region is how you control where data is stored. AWS does not move the data out of the Region on its own.","Incorrect: The billing address on the account does not decide where data is stored. The Region you choose does.","Incorrect: Edge locations cache and deliver content close to users. They are not where the original data is stored.","Incorrect: AWS does not copy data to another Region automatically. Replication between Regions is something the customer configures deliberately."]},
"d1-43":{exp:"Under the shared responsibility model, AWS secures the infrastructure (security OF the cloud), while the customer is still responsible for its data, IAM, and access configuration (security IN the cloud). The other options are real benefits: go global in minutes, pay for what you use, and stop guessing capacity.",why:["Incorrect (this is a real benefit): go global in minutes. You use existing AWS Regions instead of building facilities.","Correct (this is not a benefit): the customer still has to manage access to its own data. AWS does not do that for you.","Incorrect (this is a real benefit): trade fixed expense for variable expense. You pay only while you use it.","Incorrect (this is a real benefit): stop guessing capacity. You add capacity when you need it instead of buying in advance."]},
"d1-44":{exp:"Agility is the speed of provisioning resources and experimenting: the time from idea to having resources drops from weeks to minutes. Lower spending is an economic benefit. Less downtime and using more AZs are about reliability and high availability.",why:["Incorrect: Lower spending demonstrates an economic (cost) benefit. It does not directly measure the speed of innovation.","Incorrect: Less downtime demonstrates reliability and high availability, not agility.","Correct: Cutting the time to provision resources from weeks to minutes is the classic measure of agility.","Incorrect: Using more AZs increases high availability. It does not show that the company innovates faster."]},
"d1-45":{exp:"In the current framework, Performance Efficiency has 6 design principles: use managed services to reduce operational complexity, deploy workloads closer to your customers, build scalable architectures, experiment more often, consider mechanical sympathy, and design for change and variable demand. The trap: 'stop guessing capacity' sounds like performance but belongs to Reliability. Older documentation used the names 'democratize advanced technologies', 'go global in minutes', and 'use serverless architectures' for the first three; if you see an old name, it means the same principle.",why:["Correct: Deploying in Regions or edge locations close to users to reduce latency is a Performance Efficiency principle (old name: go global in minutes).","Correct: Architectures that scale automatically (for example, serverless) remove the work of managing servers and help you use resources efficiently. This is a Performance Efficiency principle (old name: use serverless architectures).","Correct: You can easily try different instance types and configurations to find the best-performing option.","Incorrect: 'stop guessing capacity' is a principle of the Reliability pillar (automatically adding and removing resources to match demand).","Incorrect: 'strong identity foundation' is a principle of the Security pillar.","Incorrect: 'Implement granular cost allocation' (old name: analyze and attribute expenditure) is a principle of the Cost Optimization pillar."]},
"d1-46":{exp:"Keywords: many layers of controls (edge, VPC, subnet, instance, application), which is defense in depth → 'Apply security at all layers'. Maintaining traceability is logging and monitoring. Automate means controls as code. Prepare means having an incident response process.",why:["Incorrect: Maintaining traceability means logging, monitoring, and alerting on changes in real time. It is not about adding layers of protection.","Correct: Applying security at every layer (defense in depth) means one failed layer does not expose the whole workload.","Incorrect: Automating security best practices means implementing security controls as repeatable code. The question is about the number of layers, not automation.","Incorrect: Preparing for security events means having an incident response process and practicing it. It is not about adding layers of controls."]},
"d1-47":{exp:"Well-Architected lenses extend the framework for a specific industry or technology type (Financial Services Industry, Serverless, SaaS, Machine Learning, and so on). You can apply a lens in the AWS Well-Architected Tool alongside the general pillars.",why:["Incorrect: A conformance pack is a set of Config rules that automatically check resource configurations. It is not a set of architecture review questions.","Incorrect: Trusted Advisor makes recommendations based on checks of your account. It has no industry-specific architecture guidance.","Correct: A lens provides additional questions and best practices for a specific industry or type of workload.","Incorrect: Artifact provides AWS's own compliance reports (SOC, ISO, and so on). It does not evaluate the architecture of a customer's workload."]},
"d1-48":{exp:"Well-Architected Framework: you make trade-offs between pillars depending on your business context (for example, accepting lower reliability in a dev environment to cut cost), but Security and Operational Excellence are generally not traded off.",why:["Correct: AWS advises that Security is generally not traded off for gains in another pillar.","Correct: Operational Excellence is also generally not traded off against the other pillars.","Incorrect: Cost Optimization is often traded off against Reliability or Performance depending on needs (as in this question).","Incorrect: Performance Efficiency can be traded off, which is exactly what the architects are doing to save cost.","Incorrect: Sustainability can also be weighed as a trade-off depending on the business context."]},
"d1-49":{exp:"'Implement Cloud Financial Management' is the first design principle of Cost Optimization: invest in people and processes (FinOps) to build a cost-aware culture. The trap: the organizational and training elements make it easy to confuse with Operational Excellence.",why:["Incorrect: Operational Excellence also covers organization and culture, but its goal is running operations and improving processes, not managing spending.","Correct: Cloud Financial Management (a finance plus technology team, cost awareness, spending goals) belongs to Cost Optimization.","Incorrect: Performance Efficiency focuses on using resources efficiently to meet performance requirements, not on a cost culture.","Incorrect: Sustainability aims to reduce environmental impact. The question makes it clear the goal is controlling the bill."]},
"d1-50":{exp:"Replatform ('lift, tinker and shift'): you move to a managed platform to gain a specific benefit without changing the architecture or core code. Rehost means moving as-is to EC2 and still managing the servers yourself. Relocate means moving VMware to VMware Cloud on AWS. Refactor means redesigning the application.",why:["Incorrect: Rehost means moving the servers as-is to EC2, where the team would still install and patch Tomcat. Here the platform has changed to a managed service.","Incorrect: Relocate means moving infrastructure at the hypervisor level (for example, VMware to VMware Cloud on AWS) without changing the platform.","Correct: Switching to a managed platform (Elastic Beanstalk) to reduce operational work without modifying the code is replatform.","Incorrect: Refactor requires rewriting or redesigning the application (for example, as microservices or serverless). Here the code is unchanged."]},
"d1-51":{exp:"AWS CAF has 6 perspectives: Business, People, and Governance (business capabilities) and Platform, Security, and Operations (technical capabilities). Tip: 'BPG' is the business side, 'PSO' is the technical side.",why:["Correct: Platform (technical) is about building an enterprise-grade, scalable, hybrid cloud platform.","Correct: Security (technical) is about ensuring the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of data and workloads.","Correct: Operations (technical) is about ensuring cloud services are delivered at the level the business needs.","Incorrect: Business is a business capability. It ensures cloud investments advance business goals.","Incorrect: People is a business capability. It covers culture, organizational structure, and workforce skills.","Incorrect: Governance is a business capability. It coordinates the program, maximizes benefits, and reduces risk."]},
"d1-52":{exp:"AWS CAF has 4 transformation domains: Technology (migrating and modernizing infrastructure, applications, and data), Process (digitizing, automating, and optimizing business operations, using data and ML), Organization (changing the operating model and teams), and Product (creating new value and business models).",why:["Incorrect: The Technology domain focuses on migrating and modernizing infrastructure, applications, and data platforms, not on optimizing business operations.","Correct: The Process domain is about digitizing, automating, and optimizing business operations, often with analytics and ML.","Incorrect: The Organization domain is about changing how the organization operates, for example product-based teams and agile processes.","Incorrect: The Product domain is about creating new products, services, or revenue models. The question is about optimizing existing operations."]},
"d1-53":{exp:"Rehost fits when you need to move fast, in large numbers, against a deadline (the data center lease is ending). Once applications are in the cloud, they are easier to optimize later (replatform or refactor). Rehost does not give cloud-native benefits right away, and you still have to manage the OS on EC2 yourself.",why:["Incorrect: Cloud-native benefits (serverless, microservices, and so on) come from refactoring. Rehost keeps the old architecture as it is.","Incorrect: After rehosting to EC2, the customer is still responsible for patching the guest OS.","Correct: Rehost is fast, low risk, and easy to automate at scale, which suits the five-month deadline.","Incorrect: Commercial software running on EC2 still needs a license (BYOL or license included)."]},
"d1-54":{exp:"Security perspective: achieve the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of data and workloads. Its capabilities include IAM, threat detection, incident response, data protection, and more. Governance is about managing the program and business risk. Operations is about running the services.",why:["Incorrect: Governance coordinates the cloud program and manages benefits, cost, and risk at the enterprise level. It is not about technical security controls.","Incorrect: Operations handles day-to-day running (monitoring, service incident management, changes). Security incident response belongs to Security.","Incorrect: Platform builds the cloud platform and architecture and provisions infrastructure. It does not focus on IAM and threat detection.","Correct: The Security perspective includes IAM, threat detection, and incident response to ensure the confidentiality, integrity, and availability (CIA) of data."]},
"d1-55":{exp:"Moving to AWS reduces physical infrastructure costs (hardware, electricity, cooling, floor space), and with managed services like RDS it also reduces the work of patching the database engine. The effort of writing business features, however, is the company's own unique value, so it still has to be done.",why:["Correct: Writing business features is the work that differentiates the company. Moving to the cloud does not make it go away.","Incorrect: AWS buys and refreshes the server hardware. The company no longer has this CapEx.","Incorrect: Electricity and cooling are included in the price of AWS services. The company no longer pays separately for a server room.","Incorrect: With Amazon RDS, AWS patches the database engine during the maintenance window, which reduces the company's operational work."]},
"d1-56":{exp:"AWS Marketplace has pay-as-you-go products (billed by the hour) whose software fee is added to your AWS bill. You do not need to own a license or sign a separate contract, which suits short-term use. BYOL only fits when you already have a license.",why:["Incorrect: BYOL requires the company to already have a license. The question says it owns no license.","Incorrect: License Manager only tracks and controls the use of licenses you already have. It does not sell or issue licenses.","Incorrect: Buying a perpetual license is a large upfront cost and means dealing with the vendor, which goes against the requirements.","Correct: A pay-as-you-go Marketplace listing bills the software by the hour on the AWS bill, with no separate contract."]},
"d1-57":{exp:"AWS License Manager manages software licenses (BYOL) on AWS and on premises: you set rules by vCPU, core, or socket, track usage, and can block launches that exceed the limit. It is the tool that supports a BYOL strategy.",why:["Correct: License Manager tracks how many licenses are in use and can enforce a hard limit to prevent exceeding the number of licenses owned.","Incorrect: Artifact provides AWS's compliance reports and agreements. It does not track a customer's licenses.","Incorrect: Config records and evaluates resource configurations, but it does not manage license counts or block launches that exceed a limit.","Incorrect: Systems Manager Inventory collects the list of software installed on instances, but it does not set license rules or block launches that exceed the license count."]},
"d1-58":{exp:"Rightsizing is based on actual usage data: CloudWatch provides metrics such as CPU and network over time, and Compute Optimizer uses that data plus ML to recommend the optimal instance type and size. CloudTrail records who called which API. It does not measure utilization.",why:["Correct: CloudWatch shows CPU, network, and (if you install the agent) memory utilization over time, which is the evidence you need for rightsizing.","Correct: Compute Optimizer analyzes usage history and recommends instance types that fit better.","Incorrect: CloudTrail records API calls (who did what, and when) for auditing. It does not measure how much of a resource is used.","Incorrect: The Health Dashboard reports AWS events and maintenance that affect your resources. It does not provide usage data.","Incorrect: Artifact provides AWS's compliance reports. It has nothing to do with instance sizes."]},
"d1-59":{exp:"Comparing hourly prices is not enough: an on-premises server at 15% utilization means about 85% of the capacity has been paid for but is wasted. On AWS you can rightsize, use Auto Scaling, and turn things off when they are not in use, so you pay only for the capacity you actually use. TCO also includes electricity, floor space, and staff.",why:["Incorrect: Licenses are not free. The price of a license-included instance already has the license fee built in, or the customer uses BYOL.","Correct: On premises you pay for all the capacity even when it sits idle. AWS lets you pay for actual usage, so low utilization is a big opportunity to save.","Incorrect: There is no promise of 'always costs less' per unit. The savings come from utilization and economies of scale, not from every piece of hardware being cheaper.","Incorrect: Data transferred into AWS is usually free, but data transferred out to the internet is charged."]},
"d1-60":{exp:"This is a benefit of the global infrastructure plus pay-as-you-go: the customer sets up a recovery environment in another Region without building or leasing a second data center, and pays only for the resources it uses (usually kept small until they are needed). Replicating the data is still something the customer configures.",why:["Correct: The company uses another Region on a pay-for-what-you-use model instead of investing in a second site.","Incorrect: AWS does not automatically copy a customer's systems or data to another Region.","Incorrect: There is no free standby data center. The resources you use are still charged.","Incorrect: Using AWS does not require a long-term lease for space in an AWS facility."]},
"d1-61":{exp:"The global infrastructure lets you go global in minutes (deploy quickly in many Regions) and increases high availability and fault tolerance through multiple AZs. AWS does not copy data between Regions on its own and does not promise \"never any downtime\".",why:["Correct: Speed of deployment and global reach are key benefits.","Correct: Multiple AZs help an application survive the failure of one AZ.","Incorrect: Good architecture reduces downtime, but there is no absolute guarantee.","Incorrect: Data stays in the Region the customer chooses, unless the customer configures replication.","Incorrect: Edge locations are mainly for CloudFront, Route 53, and similar services. They do not run full EC2 workloads."]},
"d1-62":{exp:"High availability: the system stays available with minimal downtime, achieved through redundancy (multiple instances, multiple AZs) and automatic recovery. The other options describe elasticity, global reach, and economies of scale.",why:["Correct: This is the definition of high availability.","Incorrect: That is elasticity.","Incorrect: That is go global in minutes.","Incorrect: That is economies of scale."]},
"d1-63":{exp:"High availability: the system is available with minimal downtime (a brief interruption during failover is possible). Fault tolerance: the system keeps running with no interruption even when a component fails, which usually needs more redundancy and costs more.",why:["Correct: HA = minimal downtime; FT = no interruption when a component fails.","Incorrect: Adding and removing resources to match demand is elasticity.","Incorrect: Protecting data and encryption belong to security and data durability, not HA or FT.","Incorrect: The two concepts are related but differ in degree. The exam can distinguish between them."]},
"d1-64":{exp:"Sustainability: maximize utilization (do not leave resources running idle), minimize the footprint of data, and reduce energy use and environmental impact. The same actions can also lower cost, but the goal stated in the question is energy.",why:["Correct: Maximizing utilization and removing unneeded data to reduce energy use is Sustainability.","Incorrect: Reliability focuses on recovering from failures and meeting demand consistently.","Incorrect: Security focuses on protecting data and systems.","Incorrect: Operational Excellence focuses on running operations and improving processes."]},
"d1-65":{exp:"Consider mechanical sympathy: use the technology approach that best fits your goals, for example by looking at data access patterns when you choose a database or storage.",why:["Correct: Choosing a technology based on how the data is accessed is mechanical sympathy.","Incorrect: Experiment more often means trying many configurations to compare them.","Incorrect: Keep people away from data is a Security principle.","Incorrect: Stop guessing capacity is a Reliability principle."]},
"d1-66":{exp:"Protect data in transit and at rest: classify data by sensitivity level and use encryption, tokenization, and appropriate access control for data both when it is stored and when it is transmitted.",why:["Correct: Classification plus encryption at rest and in transit.","Incorrect: Apply security at all layers means defense in depth (edge, VPC, instance, code).","Incorrect: Traceability means logging, monitoring, and alerting.","Incorrect: Prepare for security events means incident response processes and practice drills."]},
"d1-67":{exp:"Prepare for security events (Security): have incident management and investigation processes that match your organization's requirements, run incident response simulations, and use tools and automation to detect, investigate, and recover faster.",why:["Correct: Playbooks, tools that are ready to go, and simulated security incidents are all preparation for security events.","Incorrect: Test recovery procedures is about recovering a workload after a failure, not responding to a security incident.","Incorrect: Anticipate failure (Operational Excellence) means pre-mortems for operational failures in general. The question focuses on security incidents.","Incorrect: Experiment more often means testing configurations to optimize performance."]},
"d1-68":{exp:"Align: identify capability gaps and dependencies between departments, build strategies to improve, and get stakeholders aligned. Envision is choosing and prioritizing opportunities, Launch is the pilot, and Scale is expanding.",why:["Correct: Finding capability gaps and making plans is Align.","Incorrect: Envision is already done (the transformation opportunities have been chosen).","Incorrect: Launch means delivering pilots in production.","Incorrect: Scale means expanding the pilots across the whole organization."]},
"d1-69":{exp:"Retire: shut down applications that no longer have value, which cuts cost and security risk and shrinks the scope of the migration. Retain means keeping an application where it is (not migrating it yet) because there is still a business reason.",why:["Correct: Nobody uses the application, so it should be shut down.","Incorrect: Retain means keeping an application that is still needed.","Incorrect: Rehost means moving it as-is, which is pointless for an unused application.","Incorrect: Repurchase means switching to a different product (usually SaaS)."]},
"d1-70":{exp:"The 4 business outcomes of CAF: reduced business risk, improved ESG performance, increased revenue (new products, new markets), and increased operational efficiency (lower operating costs, higher productivity).",why:["Correct: New products and new markets increase revenue.","Incorrect: Reduced business risk means better reliability and security and lower risk.","Incorrect: ESG stands for environmental, social, and governance (for example, reducing emissions).","Incorrect: Operational efficiency means lower operating costs and higher productivity."]},
"d1-71":{exp:"CapEx: investing upfront in physical assets (servers, data centers) that are depreciated over many years. OpEx / variable expense: paying for what you use, changing with demand, like the AWS bill.",why:["Correct: The AWS bill varies with usage, so it is OpEx.","Incorrect: Buying hardware is CapEx.","Incorrect: Building data center infrastructure is CapEx.","Incorrect: Assets depreciated over several years are CapEx."]},
"d1-72":{exp:"Benefit from massive economies of scale: its huge scale lets AWS buy and operate infrastructure more cheaply and lower pay-as-you-go prices for customers.",why:["Correct: Economies of scale make lower prices possible.","Incorrect: The price reductions do not come from discontinuing services.","Incorrect: Most services are pay-as-you-go, with no long-term contract required.","Incorrect: Basic Support is free. Service prices do not go down because of support fees."]},
"d1-73":{exp:"RDS for Oracle supports BYOL (using Oracle licenses you already have) and License Included (for some editions). Reusing licenses you have already bought → BYOL, and you can track them with AWS License Manager.",why:["Correct: BYOL lets the company use the Oracle licenses it already owns.","Incorrect: License Included builds the license fee into the hourly price. It does not reuse existing licenses.","Incorrect: The Free Tier has nothing to do with existing Oracle licenses.","Incorrect: Marketplace is for buying new software, not reusing licenses you already own."]},
"d1-74":{exp:"The sensible order for cost optimization: rightsize first (pick the right size based on actual usage) → then commit (Savings Plans or RIs). Committing first locks in the cost of oversized resources.",why:["Correct: Committing after rightsizing avoids paying for capacity you do not use.","Incorrect: Savings Plans apply to many instance sizes and types.","Incorrect: AWS does not require rightsizing. It is a best practice.","Incorrect: The Free Tier has nothing to do with rightsizing."]},
"d2-70":{exp:"DynamoDB is a serverless, managed service: AWS takes care of the hardware, the software, patching, and replicating data across multiple AZs. The customer is always responsible for its data and for access (IAM policies for the tables).",why:["Correct: Managing who can access the tables (IAM) is always the customer's responsibility.","Incorrect: DynamoDB has no software for the customer to patch. AWS manages the entire engine.","Incorrect: DynamoDB automatically replicates data across multiple AZs in the Region. The customer does not have to do it.","Incorrect: The storage hardware is part of 'security of the cloud', which AWS manages."]},
"d2-71":{exp:"With Fargate, AWS manages the underlying compute infrastructure, including patching the host operating system. The customer is still responsible for the container contents (images, libraries), IAM task roles, and security groups.",why:["Correct: Fargate is serverless for containers. AWS patches and manages the hosts.","Incorrect: The contents of the image (libraries, software packages) remain the customer's responsibility, even on Fargate.","Incorrect: IAM task roles and the principle of least privilege are always the customer's job.","Incorrect: Fargate tasks still use security groups that the customer configures."]},
"d2-72":{exp:"The customer's share of responsibility shrinks as you go from IaaS (EC2) to managed services (RDS, Lambda, S3), but the customer's data and access management (IAM) are always the customer's responsibility for every service.",why:["Correct: The data always belongs to the customer. The customer decides how to classify it, encrypt it, and where to store it.","Correct: Managing identities and access is always done by the customer, even with serverless services.","Incorrect: This is true only for EC2. With RDS and Lambda, AWS patches the OS, and S3 has no OS for the customer to manage.","Incorrect: The hypervisor / virtualization layer is 'security of the cloud', which is AWS's responsibility.","Incorrect: The physical network devices in the data centers are AWS's responsibility."]},
"d2-73":{exp:"AWS automatically replicates an EBS volume within its own AZ to protect against hardware failure. That is AWS's part. Protecting the data against user error, accidental deletion, ransomware, or the loss of a whole AZ (snapshots, AWS Backup) is the customer's responsibility.",why:["Correct: Snapshots and backups are the customer's responsibility. Internal replication does not protect against accidental deletion or corrupted data.","Incorrect: AWS does not restore a volume the customer has deleted. The data is the customer's responsibility.","Incorrect: AWS already replicates the volume within the AZ. The customer does not do this themselves, and instead uses snapshots (stored in S3) to get a durable copy.","Incorrect: Encryption protects confidentiality. It cannot replace backups."]},
"d2-74":{exp:"With EC2 (IaaS), the customer manages everything from the guest OS up: OS patching, the firewall inside the OS, applications, credentials, and data encryption. The hypervisor and the physical infrastructure are 'security of the cloud', which is AWS's responsibility.",why:["Correct (this is the answer): the hypervisor is managed by AWS. The customer cannot access or patch it.","Incorrect (this is the customer's responsibility): everything inside the guest OS, including firewall software, is configured by the customer.","Incorrect (this is the customer's responsibility): managing and rotating credentials is the customer's job.","Incorrect (this is the customer's responsibility): application-level (client-side) encryption is done by the customer."]},
"d2-75":{exp:"AWS Outposts brings AWS infrastructure into the customer's own facility. AWS still manages, maintains, patches, and replaces the Outposts hardware, but the customer must provide physical security, power, networking, and a suitable environment at the site where the rack sits, which is work AWS does in a Region.",why:["Correct: The site that hosts the Outposts rack belongs to the customer, so the customer is responsible for physical security there.","Incorrect: AWS is responsible for replacing failed Outposts hardware.","Incorrect: AWS manages and updates the infrastructure software, firmware, and virtualization layer of Outposts.","Incorrect: The internal keys of AWS services are managed by AWS. The customer manages only its own keys."]},
"d2-76":{exp:"CloudTrail log file integrity validation creates digest files (SHA-256 hashes, digitally signed) so you can verify whether a log file was modified, deleted, or left unchanged since CloudTrail delivered it. Keywords 'prove the logs were not modified' → integrity validation.",why:["Correct: Digest files let you verify the integrity of each log file.","Incorrect: Versioning keeps older versions of objects, but it cannot prove whether a file was modified.","Incorrect: Config records the configuration history of resources. It does not verify the contents of log files.","Incorrect: Logs Insights is for querying and analyzing logs, not for checking integrity."]},
"d2-77":{exp:"AWS Config continuously evaluates resource configurations against rules, and you can attach remediation actions (Systems Manager Automation) to fix noncompliant resources automatically. Keywords 'continuously evaluate + automatically fix configuration' → Config rules + remediation.",why:["Correct: A Config rule detects noncompliant buckets, and remediation fixes them automatically.","Incorrect: CloudTrail Insights detects unusual API activity. It does not evaluate or fix bucket configuration.","Incorrect: Inspector scans for software vulnerabilities on EC2, container images, and Lambda. It does not check bucket access permissions.","Incorrect: Trusted Advisor can warn about open buckets, but a manual weekly review is not continuous and does not fix anything automatically."]},
"d2-78":{exp:"Encryption at rest in S3: server-side (SSE-S3, SSE-KMS, DSSE-KMS, SSE-C) or client-side (the customer encrypts before uploading). HTTPS/TLS is encryption in transit. Block Public Access and Versioning are access control and data protection features, not encryption.",why:["Correct: SSE-S3 is the default encryption, and S3 manages the keys.","Correct: SSE-KMS uses KMS keys and adds control over who can use the key, plus logging in CloudTrail.","Correct: With client-side encryption the data is stored already encrypted. The customer manages the encryption process.","Incorrect: TLS only protects data in transit.","Incorrect: Block Public Access blocks public access. It does not encrypt data.","Incorrect: Versioning keeps object versions so you can recover them. It does not encrypt data."]},
"d2-79":{exp:"An organization trail is created in the management account (or a delegated administrator account), applies automatically to every member account, including new ones, and delivers logs to one shared S3 bucket. Member accounts can see the trail but cannot modify or delete it.",why:["Correct: An organization trail logs every account, and member accounts cannot turn it off.","Incorrect: A separate trail in each account can be stopped or deleted by that account's own administrator, and new accounts are easy to miss.","Incorrect: A Config aggregator collects configuration and compliance data, not API activity logs.","Incorrect: Security Hub aggregates security findings. It does not record every API call."]},
"d2-80":{exp:"S3 Object Lock stores objects using the WORM model. Compliance mode: nobody (not even the root user) can delete or overwrite an object version, and the retention period cannot be shortened. Governance mode: people with a special permission can still bypass the lock. (Advanced — rarely appears on the real exam)",why:["Correct: Compliance mode meets the requirement that 'not even the root user can delete' the records.","Incorrect: Governance mode lets users with the s3:BypassGovernanceRetention permission delete objects or change the lock settings.","Incorrect: MFA delete only adds an authentication step. A root user with MFA can still delete object versions.","Incorrect: A Lifecycle rule only moves objects to another storage class to reduce cost. It does not prevent deletion."]},
"d2-81":{exp:"CloudTrail records the root user's ConsoleLogin event. An EventBridge rule matches this event and sends it to an SNS topic, and SNS emails the subscribers. The 'event → EventBridge → SNS' pattern is a common way to get near-real-time alerts.",why:["Correct: An EventBridge rule catches the root sign-in event that CloudTrail records.","Correct: SNS sends the email notification to the security team.","Incorrect: Inspector scans for software vulnerabilities. It does not track sign-in events.","Incorrect: Macie discovers sensitive data in S3. It does not send sign-in alerts.","Incorrect: Artifact provides AWS's compliance reports. It has nothing to do with event monitoring."]},
"d2-82":{exp:"Encryption in transit: TLS (HTTPS with an ACM certificate on the ALB) for web users, and IPsec (Site-to-Site VPN) for the on-premises ↔ VPC connection over the internet. EBS encryption and SSE-S3 are encryption at rest. Security groups only filter traffic.",why:["Correct: An HTTPS listener with an ACM certificate encrypts traffic between browsers and the ALB.","Correct: Site-to-Site VPN encrypts traffic between the on-premises data center and the VPC using IPsec.","Incorrect: EBS encryption protects data at rest on the volume. It does not encrypt network traffic.","Incorrect: SSE-S3 is encryption at rest for S3 objects.","Incorrect: Allowing port 443 is just a filtering rule. A security group itself does not encrypt anything."]},
"d2-83":{exp:"A permissions boundary is a managed policy that sets the maximum permissions for an IAM user or role: the effective permissions are the intersection of the identity policy and the boundary. Requiring developers to attach the boundary when they create a role lets you delegate safely without the limit being exceeded. (Advanced — rarely appears on the real exam)",why:["Correct: A boundary caps the maximum permissions, and even a broader policy cannot go past it.","Incorrect: Access Analyzer finds external access and helps you generate and validate policies. It does not enforce a permissions ceiling.","Incorrect: Inline policies grant permissions, they do not limit them. A developer could still attach a broader policy.","Incorrect: IAM groups grant permissions to the developers. They do not limit the permissions of the roles the developers create."]},
"d2-84":{exp:"According to the IAM documentation, 'restore IAM user permissions' is one of the tasks only the root user can perform: when the only IAM administrator locks themselves out, the root user signs in to fix the policy. This is why you must protect the root user with MFA and use it only for tasks like this.",why:["Correct: The root user has full permissions, and this is the recovery method the IAM documentation explicitly describes.","Incorrect: AWS Support does not change IAM permissions in a customer's account.","Incorrect: Access Analyzer has no feature for rolling back policy changes.","Incorrect: The user has lost their IAM permissions, so they cannot create a new user, whether through the Console or the CLI."]},
"d2-85":{exp:"Tasks only the root user can perform (current IAM documentation) include: changing account settings such as the email, root password, and root access keys; restoring IAM permissions; activating IAM access to the Billing console; closing the account; registering as a seller in the RI Marketplace; configuring MFA delete; editing a bucket policy that denies all principals; and signing up for GovCloud. Changing or canceling the Support plan is NO LONGER root-only: an IAM user or role with the right permissions can do it.",why:["Correct (this is the answer): an IAM identity with the appropriate Support plan permissions can change the Support plan.","Incorrect (requires root): MFA delete can be enabled only with the root user's credentials.","Incorrect (requires root): the root password is a setting of the root user itself.","Incorrect (requires root): signing up for AWS GovCloud (US) is a task only the root user can perform."]},
"d2-86":{exp:"An exposed key must be treated as compromised: deactivate and delete the key (create a new one if needed, or better, switch to a role with temporary credentials), then check CloudTrail to see what the key was used for. Console sign-in MFA and the password policy have no effect on access keys.",why:["Correct: Deactivating the key immediately blocks every API call that uses it.","Correct: CloudTrail shows what the key was used for and from which IP address, which helps you assess the damage.","Incorrect: A key that has been public may already have been copied. Removing the file does not revoke the key.","Incorrect: MFA for the console does not block API calls signed with the access key (unless a policy has an MFA condition).","Incorrect: The password policy applies only to console passwords. It has nothing to do with access keys."]},
"d2-87":{exp:"In IAM Identity Center, a permission set defines a set of permissions once. When you assign it to a group for multiple accounts, Identity Center automatically creates the matching role in each account. Management is centralized, and users sign in with their corporate identity.",why:["Correct: A permission set assigned to a group is the way to manage multi-account permissions centrally.","Incorrect: Creating an IAM group in each of the 15 accounts would work, but the management is spread out and does not meet the 'one place' requirement.","Incorrect: An SCP only limits the maximum permissions. It does not grant permissions to anyone.","Incorrect: IAM users in the management account have no permissions in the member accounts, and they add more long-term credentials."]},
"d2-88":{exp:"A bucket policy is a resource-based policy: it is attached directly to the resource and can name principals in other accounts. The partner account only needs to grant s3:PutObject to its own users or roles. This is how you grant cross-account access without creating identities in the company's account.",why:["Correct: A bucket policy can grant permissions to principals in another account.","Incorrect: An IAM group contains only IAM users from the same account. It cannot contain another account.","Incorrect: An SCP does not grant permissions, and it applies only to accounts in the company's own organization.","Incorrect: A permissions boundary only limits the maximum permissions of a user or role. It does not grant access to a bucket."]},
"d2-89":{exp:"IAM lets you register multiple MFA devices (up to 8) for the root user and for IAM users. Registering an additional device and keeping it in a different secure location helps you avoid being locked out without weakening security.",why:["Correct: Multiple MFA devices keep the same level of protection while giving you a backup option.","Incorrect: AWS recommends not creating access keys for the root user. Long-term root keys are a major risk.","Incorrect: Removing MFA leaves the root user protected by a password only, exactly when the risk is higher.","Incorrect: Sharing the root password violates the principle of protecting the root user, and you lose traceability."]},
"d2-90":{exp:"Security vendors sell managed rule groups for AWS WAF through AWS Marketplace. The customer subscribes and pays through the AWS bill, then adds the rule group to a web ACL. Keywords 'third party + billed through the AWS bill' → AWS Marketplace.",why:["Correct: Marketplace offers third-party managed rule groups for WAF, paid for through AWS.","Incorrect: Firewall Manager applies rules consistently across many accounts. It does not sell third-party rules.","Incorrect: Shield Advanced is an advanced DDoS protection service, not a place to buy another vendor's rule set.","Incorrect: Security Hub aggregates security findings. It does not provide rules for WAF."]},
"d2-91":{exp:"KMS is a managed, multi-tenant service (it uses FIPS-validated HSMs operated by AWS), integrates with many services, logs to CloudTrail, and supports automatic rotation. A single-tenant HSM fully controlled by the customer is AWS CloudHSM.",why:["Correct (this is the answer): dedicated single-tenant HSMs are AWS CloudHSM, not KMS.","Incorrect (KMS has this): KMS integrates with S3, EBS, RDS, and many other services.","Incorrect (KMS has this): every use of a KMS key is recorded in CloudTrail.","Incorrect (KMS has this): you can turn on automatic rotation for symmetric customer managed keys."]},
"d2-92":{exp:"AWS Shield Standard automatically protects every customer, at no extra cost, against common layer 3/4 DDoS attacks (SYN/UDP floods, reflection attacks), and it is especially effective with CloudFront and Route 53. Shield Advanced adds the SRT, cost protection, and advanced monitoring, but it comes at a significant price.",why:["Correct: Shield Standard covers the basic need and is free.","Incorrect: Shield Advanced is expensive and provides the things the owner says are not needed.","Incorrect: GuardDuty detects threats. It does not mitigate DDoS traffic.","Incorrect: Firewall Manager centrally manages rules across many accounts. It does not stop DDoS on its own and is not needed for one small account."]},
"d2-93":{exp:"Macie: discovers sensitive data (PII, PHI) in S3. GuardDuty: detects threats from logs (VPC Flow Logs, DNS, CloudTrail) using threat intelligence. Inspector: scans for software vulnerabilities on EC2, container images, and Lambda. Detective investigates, Security Hub aggregates, and Config tracks configuration.",why:["Correct: Macie finds sensitive data such as patient information in S3.","Correct: GuardDuty detects instances communicating with known malicious IP addresses.","Correct: Inspector finds unpatched software vulnerabilities (CVEs).","Incorrect: Detective helps you investigate the cause after a finding already exists. It does not detect any of the three needs itself.","Incorrect: Security Hub aggregates findings from other services. It does not scan data or vulnerabilities itself.","Incorrect: Config tracks and evaluates resource configurations. It does not scan data or detect malicious traffic."]},
"d2-94":{exp:"GuardDuty S3 Protection analyzes S3 data-plane events in CloudTrail to detect suspicious access (malicious IP addresses, unusual behavior such as downloading data with exposed credentials). Macie answers 'where is the sensitive data?', while GuardDuty answers 'who is accessing it suspiciously?'.",why:["Correct: GuardDuty detects threats to S3 data based on access activity.","Incorrect: Macie finds sensitive data and assesses bucket security. It does not detect suspicious read activity.","Incorrect: Inspector scans for software vulnerabilities on EC2, container images, and Lambda. It does not monitor S3 access.","Incorrect: Config checks bucket configuration (for example, whether it is public). It does not analyze who is reading the objects."]},
"d2-95":{exp:"With S3, AWS takes care of the infrastructure, durability, and data centers. The customer takes care of the data and access to it: bucket policies, IAM, Block Public Access, encryption choices, and versioning.",why:["Correct: Configuring access to the bucket is the customer's job.","Correct: Managing who can access the data is the customer's job.","Incorrect: The hardware is managed by AWS.","Incorrect: The durability of the service is designed and operated by AWS.","Incorrect: Physical security of the data centers is AWS's responsibility."]},
"d2-96":{exp:"Shared controls according to AWS: patch management, configuration management, and awareness and training. Physical and environmental controls are inherited controls (the customer fully inherits them from AWS).",why:["Correct: AWS configures the infrastructure, and the customer configures its own OS, databases, and applications.","Correct: AWS trains AWS employees, and the customer trains its own employees.","Incorrect: Physical and environmental controls are inherited controls, handled entirely by AWS.","Incorrect: Controlling entry to the data centers is AWS's job.","Incorrect: Destroying failed hard drives is AWS's job."]},
"d2-97":{exp:"Even though RDS is managed, the network configuration (security groups, the publicly accessible setting, subnets) and database user management are still the customer's responsibility. AWS takes care of patching the engine, the infrastructure, and the hardware.",why:["Correct: Controlling network access to the database is the customer's job.","Incorrect: A managed service does not mean AWS configures access for the customer.","Incorrect: AWS Support does not fix security configuration in a customer's account.","Incorrect: RDS can be configured as publicly accessible, and that is exactly the misconfiguration here."]},
"d2-98":{exp:"AWS's certifications (available in AWS Artifact) prove that the infrastructure and services meet the standard. The customer inherits that part but still has to configure, operate, and audit its own workload to be compliant.",why:["Correct: Compliance also follows the shared responsibility model.","Incorrect: AWS's certifications do not automatically cover the customer's configuration and applications.","Incorrect: PCI DSS applies wherever card data is processed, including in the cloud.","Incorrect: AWS does not issue certificates for customer applications. The customer has to be assessed by an auditor itself."]},
"d2-99":{exp:"Amazon Inspector: automated, continuous vulnerability management for EC2, images in ECR, and Lambda. It rescans automatically when new CVEs appear and ranks findings by risk level. Detecting threats from logs is GuardDuty, sensitive data in S3 is Macie, and blocking web attacks is WAF.",why:["Correct: Inspector scans continuously and reassesses automatically when new CVEs appear.","Correct: Inspector supports EC2, ECR, and Lambda.","Incorrect: That is Amazon GuardDuty.","Incorrect: That is Amazon Macie.","Incorrect: Inspector only detects and reports. It does not block traffic. Blocking at the edge is AWS WAF and Shield."]},
"d2-100":{exp:"AWS Security Hub collects and prioritizes findings from many security services and runs checks against standards (AWS Foundational Security Best Practices, CIS, and so on). Note: since 2025 the standards-based security posture checks are called AWS Security Hub CSPM, but the CLF-C02 exam guide still calls it AWS Security Hub.",why:["Correct: Security Hub brings findings from many services and accounts into one place, in one common standard format.","Incorrect: Detective is for investigating the root cause of an incident.","Incorrect: Firewall Manager centrally manages WAF rules, Shield, and security groups.","Incorrect: Trusted Advisor gives recommendations in 6 categories. It does not collect findings from GuardDuty, Inspector, or Macie."]},
"d2-101":{exp:"An AWS Config rule (for example, the required-tags managed rule) continuously evaluates resource configurations, marks them compliant or noncompliant, and keeps the history. CloudTrail records API calls, and CloudWatch monitors metrics and logs.",why:["Correct: A Config rule checks configuration (including tags) and keeps a compliance history.","Incorrect: CloudWatch monitors performance. It does not evaluate configuration compliance.","Incorrect: CloudTrail records who called which API. It does not evaluate compliance status.","Incorrect: Inspector scans for software vulnerabilities."]},
"d2-102":{exp:"A statement in an IAM policy mainly consists of: Effect (Allow/Deny), Action (the API actions allowed or denied), and Resource (the ARN of the resource). It can also include Condition and Principal (in resource-based policies).",why:["Correct: Effect is either Allow or Deny.","Correct: Action lists the API actions.","Correct: Resource specifies which resources the statement applies to.","Incorrect: Region is not a main element of a statement (you can restrict it through Condition).","Incorrect: Passwords are not part of a policy.","Incorrect: An access key is a credential, not a policy element."]},
"d2-103":{exp:"AWS services (Lambda, EC2, ECS, and so on) should use IAM roles to get temporary credentials that rotate automatically. For Lambda that is the execution role, which should grant only the permissions needed (least privilege).",why:["Correct: The execution role provides temporary permissions, with no secret to store.","Incorrect: Long-term access keys in environment variables are easily exposed.","Incorrect: Never use the root user for applications.","Incorrect: DynamoDB has no public mode, and granting broad access goes against best practice."]},
"d2-104":{exp:"IAM is a global service: users, groups, roles, and policies apply to the whole account in every Region. You can restrict Regions with a condition in a policy or with an SCP if you need to.",why:["Correct: IAM is not tied to a Region.","Incorrect: You do not need to re-create identities for each Region.","Incorrect: There is no copying or approval process at all.","Incorrect: Permissions depend on policies, not on the Region you are working in."]},
"d2-105":{exp:"An IAM role provides temporary security credentials that expire and are refreshed automatically (for example, through an EC2 instance profile), so you do not have to store or manually rotate long-term secrets.",why:["Correct: A role's credentials are temporary and refresh automatically.","Incorrect: That describes an IAM user's long-term access keys.","Incorrect: A role has no console password.","Incorrect: Never store credentials in source code. Roles help you avoid this."]},
"d2-106":{exp:"Trusted Advisor has an Exposed Access Keys check (in the security category) that monitors popular public code repositories to detect exposed access keys or unusual activity.",why:["Correct: Exposed Access Keys detects keys that have been made public.","Incorrect: This is a cost optimization check.","Incorrect: This is a service limits check.","Incorrect: This is a fault tolerance check."]},
"d2-107":{exp:"A network ACL evaluates rules in order from the lowest number to the highest and stops at the first rule that matches. To block that IP range, the deny rule must have a lower number than the allow rule (for example, 90). Unlike IAM, a NACL has no \"deny always wins\".",why:["Correct: Rule 100 matches first, so the request is allowed.","Incorrect: \"explicit deny wins\" is the logic of IAM policies, not NACLs.","Incorrect: NACLs support both allow and deny rules (it is security groups that have only allow rules).","Incorrect: Security groups have only allow rules, and the two layers are evaluated independently."]},
"d2-108":{exp:"GuardDuty is a managed threat detection service: it turns on quickly and applies threat intelligence and ML to CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs, and DNS logs, without you installing agents or enabling the logs yourself. GuardDuty detects and reports findings. Blocking traffic is the job of WAF, NACLs, security groups, and so on.",why:["Correct: No agents are needed for the foundational data sources, and it turns on in minutes.","Incorrect: GuardDuty comes with built-in detection logic managed by AWS.","Incorrect: GuardDuty detects. It does not block traffic at the edge on its own.","Incorrect: Finding personal data in S3 is the job of Amazon Macie."]},
"d2-109":{exp:"Inspector finds vulnerabilities. AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager automatically installs patches for the OS (and some applications) across the fleet on a schedule and reports patch compliance status. Patching the guest OS on EC2 is the customer's responsibility.",why:["Correct: Patch Manager patches in bulk on a schedule, without logging in to each instance.","Incorrect: Inspector only detects and reports vulnerabilities. It does not install patches.","Incorrect: Shield protects against DDoS.","Incorrect: Artifact provides compliance reports."]}
});
})();
